REFLECTIONS UPON THE PRESENT SITUATION.

I. The main purposes of H.M.G. in regard to Egypt are, I take it, twofold:-
a) so far as possible to transfer to a responsible Egyptian Government, resting upon popular consent, the task of the internal administration of the country and the conduct of its foreign relations;
b) while at the same time protecting our interests, which may be enumerated under the following heads:
(1) to prevent Egypt coming with the political sphere of influence of any foreign power;
(2) to secure our communications;
(3) to protect the Sudan from interference.

2. If we undertake to exclude foreign political influence from the country, we assume some measure of corresponding responsibility to prevent foreign countries acquiring any substantial cause of complaint against the Egyptian Government. (1) [below]

3. Since the declaration of February 28th, 1922, we have watched the various rivals for political power in Egypt manoeuvring for position. These manoeuvres have, of course, a long previous history, but the date mentioned opened a very definite new chapter in the struggle. The principal protagonists have been (1) the supporters of Saad Pasha Zaghlul; (2) the "Constitution al" [sic] (1) It will be noted that I do not mention the protection of minorities, a topic which does not at present appear to possess any great political reality.

