That on August 6, 1914, the Egyptian Government, by decision of the said day of the Council of Ministers, charged the Commander-in-Chief of the British troops in Egypt to provide for the defense of Egypt against any possible aggression by a power at war with His Britannic Majesty.
That he understood that by reason of his German nationality he risked being deprived of his liberty in an Egypt occupied by English troops charged with its defense and that for this reason he voluntarily left Egypt on October 10, 1914, after having entrusted the management of his bookstore to two men like him, German subjects;
That by proclamation of November 2, 1914, the Commander-in-Chief of the British forces having declared to take on the defense of Egypt, an agent of the Egyptian police of Cairo came immediately on behalf of an officer of the English Army to invite his two aforementioned men to report immediately to the said officer at the barracks.

